## sess121 — blocker 7 landed, blocker 2 redesigned (not yet written)

**Build: VERSION still `0.11.441` (landing incomplete, not revved), srcversion `C3127EAA4E9A2D59061A79D` → `AC4899E877E72D996AEF7B0`. `make modules` exits 0, no errors, no new warnings. Still STOP-SHIP; fleet stays on 0.11.440.**

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #16) rather than starting at #1, same reason as sess119/120 — the tree is mid-landing under the sess118 stop-ship ruling.

**Blocker 7 (force-release precondition) is landed.** The sole caller — the P72 orphan reclaim in `xfs_mxfs_dlm.c` — turned out to *already* establish quiescence: `state==DEMOTING` blocks the local acquire fast path and the demoter claim holds it there across the scan; `mode==NL` is load-bearing because a release sets NL only after its Phase-2 durability drain, so a stuck orphan at NL has already destaged; holder and pin counts are zero. The requirement was satisfied but unexpressed and unenforced, which is the real defect — a caller added later satisfies it by accident or not at all, and the failure is silent corruption across every slot on the probe chain.

So the caller now states what it established and the DLM refuses an incomplete statement with `-EINVAL` + `P252-FORCEREL-PRECOND`. The evidence fields are filled from values actually read under `i_dlm_lock`, not literals — that is the point; a hardcoded attestation is a false statement that should be legible at the call site. The struct lives in `include/mxfs/mxfs_dlm.h`, not `dlm_caw.h`, because the attesting caller reaches the DLM through the `v5_mount` facade and must not include the CAW header.

**Blocker 2 got a RULE-5 ruling that rejects half of what I proposed.** Intent-first publication is accepted, but my retraction argument is unsound: the owed flags are not purely a statement about on-disk state, so a clearer's read can predate a *newer* intent and erase it. Retraction needs a per-target obligation generation, and must not discharge a bit the current plan still refuses. It also found a **new release blocker in existing code** — the single overwriteable `owed_slot` cannot express two outstanding targets, so an obligation can lose its location. And the executor may not live in the BAST poll thread: a 1000-CAW retry loop there delays lock revocation and risks dependency cycles. `lreq_plan` must become side-effect-free, which is what actually closes the Q3(b) audit item.

That redesign is specified in memory but not written. Blockers 1 and 2 remain open.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, and the DLM core is mid-landing under an active stop-ship ruling that just gained a ninth item. The marker stays unwritten.

